+# Block placement performance review
+
+Reviewed files:
+
+- `src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau`
+- `src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau`
+
+## The load these scripts actually carry
+
+The server rejects any placement less than 0.1 s after a player's last accepted one (`MIN_INTERVAL`),
+so each player can place at most 10 blocks a second. With 8 players per server, that is **at most 80
+placements a second for the whole server**. On the client, `placeAt` runs once per click or tap.
+
+At that rate, each placement runs a handful of type checks, one table lookup, one vector snap, one
+distance check and one `Instance.new` call. That costs a few microseconds per call: a tiny fraction
+of a single 60 Hz frame, even with all 80 placements landing in the same second. Nobody has reported
+lag, and nothing in these scripts runs per frame or loops over a growing collection.
+
+So the question for each suggestion isn't "is this faster in theory?" It's "does this fix a
+measurable cost, and is it worth the complexity or risk it adds?" Only item 5 was worth changing,
+and the reason is simpler code, not speed.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+### 1. Pool the block Parts instead of calling `Instance.new` for every block. **Not done.**
+
+Pooling helps when objects are created and destroyed over and over: bullets, effects, debris. Here
+blocks are created and **never destroyed**. Nothing removes them, so there are no Parts to return
+to a pool, and a pool would only mean making Parts earlier than they're needed. At most 80
+`Instance.new("Part")` calls a second are trivial for the engine. A pool would add code to manage
+and save nothing.
+
+If blocks can be removed later, look at this again then. Even then, at these rates it would likely
+still not be worth it.
+
+### 2. Pack `PlaceBlock`'s arguments into a buffer to cut network traffic. **Not done.**
+
+Each call sends one short string (block names are 4–6 characters) and one `Vector3`: a few dozen
+bytes with remote overhead included. At the 10/s cap that is well under 1 KB/s per player, which is
+negligible next to the character and physics replication Roblox already does. (The client can fire
+faster than 10/s if it spams clicks, since the cap is enforced on the server, but the payload is
+still tiny.)
+
+A buffer would also make things worse:
+
+- The server would have to check the buffer's length and decode it by hand before it could use the
+  values. The current `typeof(...) == "string"` / `"Vector3"` checks are simple and hard to get wrong.
+- Encoding and decoding cost CPU on both sides to save bytes that don't matter.
+- The remote becomes harder to read and to debug in the network tools.
+
+If bandwidth ever matters, the cheaper fix is to send a block type index (a number) instead of the
+name. That isn't needed now either.
+
+### 3. Change `PlaceBlock` to an `UnreliableRemoteEvent`. **Not done. This would cause bugs.**
+
+Unreliable remotes aren't "faster" in any way that helps here. They skip resending and ordering, so
+**events can be dropped or arrive out of order**. They're meant for high-frequency, replaceable data
+where only the latest value matters, like cursor positions or cosmetic effects. A block placement is
+a one-off action the player expects to happen. On an unreliable remote, some clicks would just do
+nothing, mostly for players on worse connections. That would be a real bug, created to fix a
+problem we don't have. Unreliable events also have a payload size limit, which is another thing to
+keep in mind for no benefit.
+
+The latency gain only shows up when packets are being lost, and that's exactly when unreliable
+events drop the placement.
+
+### 4. Put `--!native` at the top of both scripts. **Not done.**
+
+Native code generation speeds up Luau that does a lot of computation: math loops, buffer
+processing, procedural generation. These scripts mostly call into the engine (`Instance.new`,
+property sets, `Raycast`, `FireServer`, `SetAttribute`), and native compilation doesn't make those
+engine calls any faster. The Luau they do run is a few comparisons per event. There's nothing for
+native mode to speed up that anyone would notice, and it adds compile time and memory for these
+scripts. Save `--!native` for code that profiling shows is computation-heavy.
+
+### 5. Replace the `table.find` check with a lookup table. **Done, but to simplify the code, not for speed.**
+
+At 5 block types, `table.find` over a 5-element array is effectively free, so this was never a
+performance problem. But the lookup table **already existed**: `BlockTypes.ByName` (frozen, built in
+`BlockTypes.luau`). The server built a second copy of the names (`VALID_NAMES`), searched it, and
+then indexed `ByName` anyway. That is two sources of truth for one check.
+
+Change: removed `VALID_NAMES` and its setup loop. The handler now does:
+
+```lua
+local blockType = BlockTypes.ByName[blockTypeName]
+if not blockType then
+	return
+end
+```
+
+This behaves exactly like before: only names in `BlockTypes.List` pass, and the string type check
+still runs first. `ByName` is a plain frozen table with no metatable, so arbitrary client strings
+can't match anything unexpected. It also narrows `blockType` to non-nil for the type checker.
+
+### 6. Create the `RaycastParams` once instead of on every click. **Not done.**
+
+`placeAt` runs once per click or tap, which is a human input rate. Making one `RaycastParams` and a
+one-element table per click costs nothing measurable.
+
+Caching it would also bring in a correctness trap. The filter excludes the **current** character,
+and the character is replaced every time the player respawns. A cached params object would have to
+be refreshed on `CharacterAdded`, or have `FilterDescendantsInstances` reset on every click anyway.
+Otherwise, after a respawn, clicks could hit the player's own new character. Building it fresh each
+click is simpler, and it's correct by construction.
+
+## Summary
+
+| # | Suggestion | Verdict |
+|---|------------|---------|
+| 1 | Pool Parts | No: blocks are never destroyed, so nothing to reuse |
+| 2 | Buffer-pack arguments | No: payload is already tiny, and it makes validation harder |
+| 3 | UnreliableRemoteEvent | No: would silently drop placements |
+| 4 | `--!native` | No: code mostly calls the engine, nothing computation-heavy to speed up |
+| 5 | Lookup table instead of `table.find` | Yes: reuse the existing `BlockTypes.ByName` (simpler, one source of truth) |
+| 6 | Reuse `RaycastParams` | No: per-click cost is nothing, and caching risks a stale character filter |
+
+## If performance ever does become a concern
+
+Since blocks are never removed, the thing that grows over a long session is the **number of Parts in
+`Workspace.Blocks`** (and the `occupied` table). Thousands of anchored Parts affect rendering,
+replication to players who join, and memory far more than anything on the list above. If long
+sessions start to feel slow, profile with the MicroProfiler and the Developer Console's memory and
+network views first. Likely fixes then are a per-server block cap, removing blocks, or streaming. Not
+micro-optimizing the placement handler.
